  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"This book is dedicated to all the people, places, and institutions who helped me transition at such little cost. 'How to transition on sixty-three cents a day' was written, designed, typeset by hand and printed on a platen letterpress by Lee Krist. It was printed at the EM Space Book Arts Center and at the IPRC in Portland, Oregon, from 2005 to 2013"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Thirty-one postcards with Oregon themes on one side and letterpress printed text on the other, a coffee punch card, a card created to resemble the Monopoly game's "Get out of jail free" card, a sheet letterpress printed with a letter from a friend of the artist, a sheet letterpress printed with a message from the artist's doctor, a torn-out fashion magazine advertisement with added letterpress printed text, a folded sheet with letterpress printed text, and a short filmstrip with letterpress printed and handwritten text, all issued unbound in a 35 mm film canister. Colophon is attached to the inside of the bottom half of the film canister. Two title labels and an Oregon-themed sticker are attached to the outside of the lid of the canister. Crumpled-up paper restaurant menus are used to keep the cards, sheets, and filmstrip in place inside the canister.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"This unbound book is a memoir with a non-linear narrative that unfolds through thirty postcards from the author to his mother. This epistolary tells the tale of the author moving from New York City to Portland, Oregon, and physically transitioning from female to male"--Artist's statement at the Vamp &amp; Tramp Booksellers' website, viewed on June 1, 2016.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"A Key component of 'How to...' and the intent of Lee Krist is for it to serve a much needed and lacking resource for trans-queer individuals, particularly youth who may be struggling with the economic and familial challenges that may accompany an immense life-changing process as transitioning"--Statement from the Puffin Foundation at the Vamp &amp; Tramp Booksellers' website, viewed on June 1, 2016.</subfield>
  </datafield>
